[2026-07-20T17:19:30.484Z] [INFO]         "content": "Web search results for query: \"playwright chromium bundled electron app macOS notarization codesign framework symlink \"Chrome for Testing\"\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Code Signing | Electron\",\"url\":\"https://www.electronjs.org/docs/latest/tutorial/code-signing\"},{\"title\":\"How to code-sign and notarize an Electron application for macOS | BigBinary Blog\",\"url\":\"https://www.bigbinary.com/blog/code-sign-notorize-mac-desktop-app\"},{\"title\":\"Signing a macOS app | Electron Forge\",\"url\":\"https://www.electronforge.io/guides/code-signing/code-signing-macos\"},{\"title\":\"Electron | Playwright\",\"url\":\"https://playwright.dev/docs/api/class-electron\"},{\"title\":\"Installing Chromium for Playwright on Mac - Technoverse\",\"url\":\"https://technoverse.id/en/2024/12/installing-chromium-for-playwright-on-mac/\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - electron/notarize: Notarize your macOS Electron Apps · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/electron/notarize\"},{\"title\":\"How to Publish Desktop Apps on macOS in 2026 (Electron, Notarization & Beyond)\",\"url\":\"https://www.forasoft.com/blog/article/the-pain-of-publishing-electron-apps-on-macos-303\"},{\"title\":\"electron/notarize\",\"url\":\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/@electron/notarize\"},{\"title\":\"electron builder.Interface.MacConfiguration - electron-builder\",\"url\":\"https://www.electron.build/electron-builder.Interface.MacConfiguration.html\"}]\n\nI've performed a web search on the topics you requested. Here's what I found:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n**Playwright and Chromium on macOS:**\nChromium developers don't follow a process called notarization, which is a process to register your application to Apple for checking to verify that no malicious component is present. This is a significant issue for developers using bundled Chromium with Playwright on macOS.\n\n**Electron App Code Signing & Notarization:**\nOn macOS, there are two layers of security technology for application distribution: code signing and notarization. Code Signing is the act of certifying the identity of the app's author and ensuring it was not tampered with before distribution. Notarization is an extra verification step where the app is sent to Apple servers for an automated malware scan. From macOS 10.15 (Catalina) onwards, your application needs to be both code signed and notarized to run on a user's machine without disabling additional operating system security checks.\n\n**Framework and Binary Signing:**\nEvery part of the app that ships as a binary has to be individually signed with the Hardened Runtime, including native Node modules, Electron Framework helpers, Squirrel update helpers, and any bundled CLI tools. One unsigned dylib somewhere in node_modules/ will fail notarisation with a cryptic log file.\n\n**Tools Available:**\nElectron Forge uses the @electron/osx-sign tool to sign your macOS application. and Electron Forge uses the @electron/notarize tool to notarize your macOS application.\n\nThe search results don't contain specific information about \"Chrome for Testing,\" symlink handling, or framework-specific workarounds. Would you like me to search for more specific information about those topics?\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."
