[2026-07-20T17:22:36.481Z] [INFO]         "text": "This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\nSummary:\n1. **Primary Request and Intent:**\n\n   The user gave a single directive to solve GitHub issue https://github.com/link-assistant/formal-ai/issues/812 (\"Check for all false positives, false negatives, warnings and errors in CI/CD and fix them all\") in working directory `/tmp/gh-issue-solver-1784567326843`, on prepared branch `issue-812-7f9674a82402`, updating the already-existing PR https://github.com/link-assistant/formal-ai/pull/813.\n\n   Explicit requirements from the user message:\n   - \"Download all logs and collect data related about the issue to this repository, and compile that data into the ./dev/log/issues/812/pulls/813 folder.\"\n   - \"Use the collected evidence to do a deep analysis (search online for additional facts and data), reconstruct the timeline/sequence of events, list each and every requirement from the issue, find the root cause of each problem, and propose possible solutions and solution plans for each requirement. Also check online for known existing components/libraries that solve a similar problem or can help.\"\n   - \"If there is not enough data to find the actual root cause, add debug output and a verbose mode (if not already present) so the root cause can be found on the next iteration. Keep the default state switched off.\"\n   - \"If the issue is related to another repository/project, report issues on GitHub for that project when possible. Each report must contain reproducible examples, workarounds, and suggestions for fixing the issue in code.\"\n   - \"Double-check that the requirements are fully applied to the entire codebase: if an issue exists in multiple places, apply it in all of them.\"\n\n   Constraints stated in the environment/guidelines: push only to branch `issue-812-7f9674a82402`; PR 813 already exists (update, don't create); run local CI checks before pushing; commit atomic steps; use `gh pr ready 813` when finished; save large logs to files; read files >1500 lines in chunks; keep debug/test scripts in `./examples` and/or `./experiments`.\n\n   The issue body itself adds requirements: compare against `link-foundation/{rust,js,python}-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template` (full file tree), report issues upstream in the templates if the same defect is found there, follow `https://github.com/link-assistant/hive-mind/blob/main/docs/CI-CD-BEST-PRACTICES.md`, and \"plan and execute everything in this single pull request … until it is each and every requirement fully addressed\".\n\n2. **Key Technical Concepts:**\n   - GitHub Actions CI/CD (`release.yml` = \"CI/CD Pipeline\", `desktop-release.yml` = \"Desktop Release\"), workflow_run triggers, job-level `if:`/`timeout-minutes`/concurrency/permissions\n   - `rust-script` standalone scripts in `scripts/*.rs`, included into tests via `#[path = ...] mod`\n   - Links Notation (`.lino`) ledger format, parsed by `links_notation::parse_lino`\n   - Self-hosting metric: `Formal-AI-Session` / `Formal-AI-Evidence` commit trailers, basis-point arithmetic, trailing-window weighted ratchet\n   - `EvidencePolicy::{Strict, Lenient}` precedent from issue #810 (strict at PR gate, lenient at release recording)\n   - electron-builder / `@electron/osx-sign` ad-hoc codesigning, embedded `.framework` sealing rules\n   - `git rev-list --reverse --no-merges`, `git show --format= --numstat --no-renames`\n   - GitHub workflow command annotations (`::warning title=...::`)\n\n3. **Files and Code Sections:**\n\n   - **`scripts/self-hosting-metric.rs`** (520 lines originally; the primary file being modified). Contains `measure`, `measure_with_policy`, `changed_lines_for_commit`, `commit_has_formal_ai_evidence`, `trailer_values`, `record_release`, `weighted_percentage`, `read_release_rows`, `parse_release_row`, `append_release_row`, `release_note_for_tag`. Edits applied so far:\n\n     a) Module doc updated: \"merge commits, binary files and captured artifacts (see [`is_non_authored_path`]) do not contribute.\"\n\n     b) New constants after `DEFAULT_TRAILING_WINDOW`:\n     ```rust\n     /// Measurement-definition epoch of the rows this build writes.\n     ///\n     /// Issue #812: version 1 counted every changed line, including the captured CI\n     /// logs that `dev/log/issues/<n>/pulls/<n>/` bundles hold. In the `v0.300.0..HEAD`\n     /// range that was **94.58% of the whole denominator** (601 765 of 636 240 lines,\n     /// measured by `experiments/self_hosting_ratchet_replay/replay.py`), so the\n     /// published share described log volume rather than authored work — in both\n     /// directions. Version 2 excludes captured artifacts.\n     ///\n     /// Rows measured under different definitions are not comparable, so the ratchet\n     /// and the trailing window only ever look at rows of the *same* version. A\n     /// definition change therefore starts a new epoch instead of silently averaging\n     /// two different quantities.\n     const METRIC_VERSION: u64 = 2;\n\n     /// File extensions whose content is captured, not authored: CI run logs, agent\n     /// transcripts, saved diffs and process output. Committing them is deliberate\n     /// (they are the evidence bundles this repository requires), but they are not\n     /// release work and must not move the metric.\n     const CAPTURED_ARTIFACT_EXTENSIONS: &[&str] =\n         &[\"log\", \"jsonl\", \"diff\", \"patch\", \"stderr\", \"stdout\"];\n\n     /// Dependency lockfiles: written by a package manager, never hand-authored.\n     const LOCKFILE_NAMES: &[&str] = &[\n         \"Cargo.lock\",\n         \"bun.lock\",\n         \"package-lock.json\",\n         \"yarn.lock\",\n         \"pnpm-lock.yaml\",\n         \"poetry.lock\",\n         \"uv.lock\",\n     ];\n     ```\n\n     c) `ReleaseRow` gained a first field:\n     ```rust\n     pub struct ReleaseRow {\n         /// Measurement epoch this row was produced under; see [`METRIC_VERSION`].\n         /// Rows written before issue #812 carry no field and read back as `1`.\n         pub metric_version: u64,\n         pub tag: String,\n         ...\n     ```\n\n     d) New public predicate + its use inside `changed_lines_for_commit`:\n     ```rust\n     /// Whether a path holds captured output rather than authored work.\n     ///\n     /// Applied symmetrically to the numerator and the denominator, so a commit that\n     /// only files evidence contributes nothing either way and the share it reports\n     /// is unchanged by how much log volume happened to be attached to it.\n     pub fn is_non_authored_path(path: &str) -> bool {\n         // `git show --numstat` C-quotes any path with non-printable or non-ASCII\n         // bytes, so the raw field can arrive wrapped in double quotes.\n         let path = path.trim().trim_matches('\"');\n         let name = path.rsplit('/').next().unwrap_or(path);\n         if LOCKFILE_NAMES.contains(&name) {\n             return true;\n         }\n         name.rsplit_once('.').is_some_and(|(_, extension)| {\n             CAPTURED_ARTIFACT_EXTENSIONS\n                 .iter()\n                 .any(|candidate| extension.eq_ignore_ascii_case(candidate))\n         })\n     }\n     ```\n     and inside the `try_fold` closure, after reading additions/deletions:\n     ```rust\n     let path = fields.next().unwrap_or_default();\n     if additions == \"-\" || deletions == \"-\" {\n         return Ok(total);\n     }\n     if is_non_authored_path(path) {\n         return Ok(total);\n     }\n     ```\n\n     e) New `RatchetPolicy` enum with a long doc comment citing run 29751001867 log line 23918 and the #796/#810/#812 outage series; `record_release` now delegates:\n     ```rust\n     #[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, Eq, PartialEq)]\n     pub enum RatchetPolicy {\n         Enforce,\n         Report,\n     }\n\n     pub fn record_release(repo, ledger, tag, since, until, trailing_window) -> Result<ReleaseRow, String> {\n         record_release_with_policy(repo, ledger, tag, since, until, trailing_window, RatchetPolicy::Enforce)\n     }\n\n     pub fn record_release_with_policy(\n         repo: &Path, ledger: &Path, tag: &str, since: &str, until: &str,\n         trailing_window: usize, ratchet: RatchetPolicy,\n     ) -> Result<ReleaseRow, String> {\n         ...\n         let all_rows = read_release_rows(ledger)?;\n         // Only rows measured under the current definition are comparable; see\n         // `METRIC_VERSION`. Mixing epochs would average two different quantities.\n         let rows = all_rows.iter()\n             .filter(|row| row.metric_version == METRIC_VERSION)\n             .cloned().collect::<Vec<_>>();\n         ...\n         let mut row = ReleaseRow { metric_version: METRIC_VERSION, tag: tag.to_owned(), ... };\n     ```\n\n     f) Ratchet decision block replaced:\n     ```rust\n     // Idempotency is checked against the whole ledger, not just this epoch: a\n     // tag must never be recorded twice, whatever definition produced it.\n     if let Some(existing) = all_rows.iter().find(|existing| existing.tag == tag) {\n         if existing == &row { return Ok(existing.clone()); }\n         return Err(format!(\"release {tag} already has a different self-hosting ledger row\"));\n     }\n     if let Some(regression) = ratchet_regression(rows.last(), &row) {\n         match ratchet {\n             RatchetPolicy::Enforce => return Err(regression),\n             RatchetPolicy::Report => {\n                 eprintln!(\"warning: {regression}\");\n                 println!(\"::warning title=Self-hosting ratchet fell::{regression}\");\n             }\n         }\n     }\n     append_release_row(ledger, &row)?;\n     Ok(row)\n     }\n\n     /// The ratchet message when `row` regresses against `previous`, else `None`.\n     fn ratchet_regression(previous: Option<&ReleaseRow>, row: &ReleaseRow) -> Option<String> {\n         let previous = previous?;\n         if row.trailing_percentage_basis_points >= previous.trailing_percentage_basis_points {\n             return None;\n         }\n         Some(format!(\n             \"self-hosting ratchet would fall from {} to {} for {}\",\n             format_percentage(previous.trailing_percentage_basis_points),\n             format_percentage(row.trailing_percentage_basis_points),\n             row.tag\n         ))\n     }\n     ```\n\n     g) `parse_release_row` gained backward-compatible epoch parsing:\n     ```rust\n     let optional_number = |key: &str| -> Result<Option<u64>, String> {\n         match value(key) {\n             Ok(raw) => raw.parse::<u64>().map(Some)\n                 .map_err(|error| format!(\"release row has invalid {key}: {error}\")),\n             Err(_) => Ok(None),\n         }\n     };\n     Ok(ReleaseRow {\n         // Rows recorded before issue #812 carry no epoch marker; they are, by\n         // definition, epoch 1.\n         metric_version: optional_number(\"metric_version\")?.unwrap_or(1),\n         tag: value(\"tag\")?,\n         ...\n     ```\n\n     h) `append_release_row` format string now writes `metric_version` first (most recent edit, just applied):\n     ```rust\n     \"  release\\n    metric_version \\\"{}\\\"\\n    tag \\\"{}\\\"\\n    since \\\"{}\\\"\\n    until \\\n      \\\"{}\\\"\\n    self_authored_lines \\\"{}\\\"\\n    changed_lines \\\"{}\\\"\\n    \\\n      self_authored_commits \\\"{}\\\"\\n    commits \\\"{}\\\"\\n    percentage_basis_points \\\"{}\\\"\\n  \\\n      \\  trailing_window \\\"{}\\\"\\n    trailing_percentage_basis_points \\\"{}\\\"\",\n     row.metric_version,\n     row.tag,\n     ...\n     ```\n\n   - **`experiments/self_hosting_ratchet_replay/replay.py`** (created): standalone Python reimplementation of the metric that prints per-commit attribution + reason + subject, sorted by line count, then the aggregate. Used to reproduce and diagnose without rust-script.\n\n   - **`data/meta/self-hosting-ledger.lino`**: header uses 2-space indent (`attribution_policy`, `session_trailer`, `evidence_trailer`, `line_unit \"added_and_deleted\"`, `default_trailing_window \"3\"`); rows are `  release` with 4-space-indented fields. 11 existing rows, v0.296.0 → v0.300.0. Last row: v0.300.0, 141380/203258, pct 6956, trailing 3268.\n\n   - **`scripts/version-and-commit.rs:506-530`** `record_self_hosting_release` — calls `record_release(&repo, &ledger, &tag, &since, \"HEAD\", 3)`; **must be changed to `record_release_with_policy(..., RatchetPolicy::Report)`** (not yet done).\n\n   - **`.github/workflows/release.yml:124-148`** `evidence-check` job (PR-only) — runs `rust-script scripts/self-hosting-metric.rs --since \"$merge_base\" --until HEAD`; this is where ratchet enforcement should be wired via a new `--check-ratchet` mode (not yet done).\n\n   - **`tests/unit/specification/self_hosting_metric.rs`** (224 lines) — 4 tests: `recorded_formal_ai_evidence_drives_the_release_metric_and_ratchet` (expects 3/4 = 75.00% and a ratchet Err), `trailers_are_recognized_even_when_separated_by_blank_lines`, `a_malformed_historical_evidence_record_cannot_deadlock_a_release`, `release_pipeline_and_ledger_remain_pinned_to_the_metric`. Fixture ledger header written inline in `fixture_repo()`.\n\n   - **`REQUIREMENTS.md:1240-1247`** — R473–R479. R474 and R478 both need amendment for issue #812.\n\n   - **`dev/log/issues/810/pulls/811/analysis.md`** (384 lines) — prior iteration's analysis; Defect B (macOS) left open by design with instrumentation; section 7 template sweep with two still-open items (js template `check-file-line-limits` covering `.md`/`release.yml`; `simulate-fresh-merge.sh` not run in desktop-release.yml).\n\n   - **`dev/log/issues/812/pulls/813/`** — created with `ci-logs/` (run-29751001867.{log,json}, run-29752745259.{log,json}), `issue/` (issue-812.json, issue-812-comments.json — empty array), `github/` (pr-813.json, three comment/review JSONs — all empty arrays, main-runs.json), `analysis/self-hosting-replay-v0.301.0.txt`, plus empty `templates/`, `research/`.\n\n4. **Errors and fixes:**\n   - `jq: command not found` → used `gh ... --jq` instead.\n   - `cd dev/log/...` failed because the shell CWD had changed → used absolute paths thereafter.\n   - `rust-script` not installed → `cargo install rust-script` in background (succeeded).\n   - Python ledger parser `KeyError: 'tag'` because header fields precede the first `release` line → rewrote the parser to only collect fields after a `release` line.\n   - No user feedback/corrections received (only one user message so far).\n\n5. **Problem Solving:**\n\n   **Defect A — Auto Release blocked (CI/CD Pipeline run 29751001867, log line 23918):** `self-hosting ratchet would fall from 32.68% to 18.24% for v0.301.0`, exit 1. Reproduced locally byte-exact with both the Python replay and the real rust-script. Two independent root causes established with evidence:\n   1. *Denominator pollution:* 601,765 of 636,240 changed lines (94.58%) in the v0.300.0..main window are under `dev/log/`; by extension 549,412 `.log`, 41,792 `.diff`, 6,891 `.jsonl`, only 2,364 `.md`. The metric therefore measures committed CI-log volume, not authored work — a false positive in the published numbers too (v0.300.0's recorded 69.53% becomes 5.39% once artifacts are excluded). Recursive aggravation: the very act of complying with this issue (\"compile logs into dev/log/…\") guarantees the next window is also log-dominated.\n   2. *Non-actionable gate:* even with artifacts excluded, the recomputed trailing share still falls (v0.300.0 = 16.55% → v0.301.0 = 13.39%), so the release stays blocked. The ratchet runs on `main` after every contributing commit is immutable; the only way to move the range forward is cutting a tag, which is exactly what it blocks. Third outage of this class (#796, #810, #812).\n\n   Chosen fix: (i) exclude captured artifacts + lockfiles symmetrically from numerator and denominator; (ii) `METRIC_VERSION = 2` epoch so ratchet/trailing window never compare rows from different definitions (no history rewrite needed — v0.301.0 becomes the first epoch-2 row, so it has no predecessor to regress against); (iii) `RatchetPolicy::Report` at release time (record + `::warning`), `RatchetPolicy::Enforce` at the PR gate where it is actionable.\n\n   **Defect B — macOS desktop builds (Desktop Release run 29752745259):** `codesign` fails on `Contents/Resources/browser-runtime/Frameworks/Google Chrome for Testing Framework.framework` — \"unsealed contents present in the root directory of an embedded framework\"; `Publish SHA256SUMS.txt + provenance` then fails as designed. Prior iteration proved the custom sign hook and its `ignore` predicate ARE consulted and DO skip that path, so the failing invocation comes from elsewhere. Delegated to a background subagent.\n\n   **Requirement 7 — template sweep:** delegated to a second background subagent.\n\n6. **All user messages:**\n   - (System-reminder context, not a user turn: user email `link.assistant.team@proton.me`, date 2026-07-20.)\n   - The single genuine user message: \"Issue to solve: https://github.com/link-assistant/formal-ai/issues/812 / Your prepared branch: issue-812-7f9674a82402 / Your prepared working directory: /tmp/gh-issue-solver-1784567326843 / Your prepared Pull Request: https://github.com/link-assistant/formal-ai/pull/813 — Download all logs and collect data related about the issue to this repository, and compile that data into the ./dev/log/issues/812/pulls/813 folder. Use the collected evidence to do a deep analysis (search online for additional facts and data), reconstruct the timeline/sequence of events, list each and every requirement from the issue, find the root cause of each problem, and propose possible solutions and solution plans for each requirement. Also check online for known existing components/libraries that solve a similar problem or can help. If there is not enough data to find the actual root cause, add debug output and a verbose mode (if not already present) so the root cause can be found on the next iteration. Keep the default state switched off. If the issue is related to another repository/project, report issues on GitHub for that project when possible. Each report must contain reproducible examples, workarounds, and suggestions for fixing the issue in code. Double-check that the requirements are fully applied to the entire codebase: if an issue exists in multiple places, apply it in all of them. Proceed.\"\n   - (A background-task notification about rust-script install completing — explicitly NOT user input.)\n\n7. **Pending Tasks:**\n   - #1 Collect CI evidence into dev/log/issues/812/pulls/813 (largely done; analysis writeup pending)\n   - #2 Fix self-hosting metric denominator (in progress — edits applied, not compiled/tested)\n   - #3 Move ratchet enforcement to where it is actionable (partially done in the metric script; `version-and-commit.rs` call site and `release.yml` `--check-ratchet` wiring still to do)\n   - #4 Fix macOS desktop codesign failure (awaiting subagent report)\n   - #5 Apply CI/CD template sweep findings (awaiting subagent report)\n   - #6 Write analysis.md, regression tests, changelog fragment, REQUIREMENTS.md update, run local CI, update PR 813 description, `gh pr ready 813`\n\n8. **Current Work:**\n\n   Mid-way through editing `scripts/self-hosting-metric.rs`. Eight edits have been applied (module doc, `METRIC_VERSION`/`CAPTURED_ARTIFACT_EXTENSIONS`/`LOCKFILE_NAMES` constants, `ReleaseRow.metric_version`, `is_non_authored_path` + its use in `changed_lines_for_commit`, numstat quote stripping, `RatchetPolicy` + `record_release_with_policy` + epoch filtering, `ratchet_regression`, `parse_release_row` optional epoch, and — the most recent edit — `append_release_row` now writing `metric_version \"{}\"` as the row's first field).\n\n   Nothing has been compiled, tested, or committed yet