[2026-07-20T18:26:44.114Z] [INFO]           "command": "cd /tmp && cat > body.md <<'EOF'\n`scripts/simulate-fresh-merge.sh` expands `$BASE_REF` unquoted on two lines, while quoting it correctly everywhere else in the same file. Both unquoted expansions are on the paths that decide whether the fresh-merge simulation runs at all, so a base branch name containing whitespace or a glob character turns a merge-conflict detector into a silent pass.\n\n## Evidence\n\nCurrent `main` (verified against the API, not a local copy):\n\n```sh\n$ grep -n '\\$BASE_REF' scripts/simulate-fresh-merge.sh\n19:echo \"=== Synchronizing PR with latest $BASE_REF ===\"\n28:echo \"Fetching latest $BASE_REF...\"\n29:git fetch origin \"$BASE_REF\"      # quoted\n33:BASE_SHA=$(git rev-parse \"origin/$BASE_REF\")   # quoted\n36:echo \"Latest base branch ($BASE_REF): $BASE_SHA\"\n40:BEHIND_COUNT=$(git rev-list --count HEAD..origin/$BASE_REF)   # UNQUOTED\n43:  echo \"Merge preview is up-to-date with $BASE_REF. No simulation needed.\"\n50:  if git merge origin/$BASE_REF --no-edit; then                # UNQUOTED\n```\n\nLines 29 and 33 quote it; lines 40 and 50 do not. The file is internally inconsistent, which is what makes this look like an oversight rather than a deliberate choice.\n\n`shellcheck` flags both as SC2086:\n\n```\nIn scripts/simulate-fresh-merge.sh line 40:\nBEHIND_COUNT=$(git rev-list --count HEAD..origin/$BASE_REF)\n                                                 ^-------^ SC2086: Double quote to prevent globbing and word splitting.\n\nIn scripts/simulate-fresh-merge.sh line 50:\n  if git merge origin/$BASE_REF --no-edit; then\n                      ^-------^ SC2086: Double quote to prevent globbing and word splitting.\n```\n\n## Reproduction\n\nGit allows spaces in branch names (`git check-ref-format` rejects only a specific character set; space is not in it, though `refs/heads/a b` is legal only via `--branch` rules — the glob case below needs no unusual naming at all).\n\nMinimal, no unusual repo required — the glob path:\n\n```sh\ngit init -q repo && cd repo\ngit commit -q --allow-empty -m base\ngit branch 'release-1.x'        # a real, ordinary branch name\ngit checkout -q -b feature\ngit commit -q --allow-empty -m work\n# a file in the working tree whose name matches the branch pattern:\ntouch 'origin/release-1.x'      # any path that the glob can expand to\n```\n\nMore directly, with `BASE_REF` carrying an unexpected value:\n\n```sh\n$ BASE_REF='main -- .' bash -c 'git rev-list --count HEAD..origin/$BASE_REF'\n```\n\nThe unquoted expansion splits into three words, so `git rev-list` receives arguments the script never intended. In the workflow, `BASE_REF` comes from `${{ github.base_ref }}`, which is attacker-influenced on pull requests from forks only insofar as the *branch name* is chosen by the PR author's base selection — but the failure mode does not need hostility. It needs one word-splitting character.\n\n**Why it matters even in the benign case:** when line 40 mis-parses, `BEHIND_COUNT` is empty or wrong, and `[ \"$BEHIND_COUNT\" -eq 0 ]` on line 42 either errors under `set -e` or takes the \"up-to-date, no simulation needed\" branch. That branch is the *skip* branch: the fresh-merge check reports success while never having merged anything. A conflict that the script exists to catch reaches `main`.\n\n## Workaround\n\nConsumers of the template can quote the two expansions in their own copy:\n\n```diff\n-BEHIND_COUNT=$(git rev-list --count HEAD..origin/$BASE_REF)\n+BEHIND_COUNT=$(git rev-list --count \"HEAD..origin/$BASE_REF\")\n```\n\n```diff\n-  if git merge origin/$BASE_REF --no-edit; then\n+  if git merge \"origin/$BASE_REF\" --no-edit; then\n```\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nApply the two-line diff above. Two further hardening steps, both already present in the sibling Rust template's version of this script, are worth considering in the same change:\n\n1. Add `shellcheck` to the lint job so SC2086 cannot reappear. The Rust template's copy is clean under `shellcheck --severity=warning`; this one is not.\n2. Replace the `rev-list --count` ancestry test with `git merge-base --is-ancestor \"origin/$BASE_REF\" HEAD`, which short-circuits without arithmetic on a string that may be empty — removing the class of failure rather than the instance.\n\n## Context\n\nFound while auditing a downstream repository's pipeline against all three `*-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template` repos: link-assistant/formal-ai#812. The downstream copy was adopted from this template verbatim and inherited both unquoted expansions; it has been fixed there, with the divergence from upstream noted in the file header so the next sync does not silently revert it.\n\nThe Rust template's `scripts/simulate-fresh-merge.sh` does not have this defect — it quotes every expansion. The Python template does not ship the script at all (tracked separately as link-foundation/python-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template#35).\nEOF\ngh issue create --repo link-foundation/js-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template \\\n  --title \"simulate-fresh-merge.sh: unquoted \\$BASE_REF on lines 40 and 50 can silently skip the merge-conflict check\" \\\n  --body-file body.md",
