[2026-07-20T19:01:01.424Z] [INFO]     "stdout": "**1. `Auto Release` deadlocked on the self-hosting ratchet.** Two independent root causes:\n\n- *The denominator measured log volume, not authorship.* 599,340 of the 636,240 lines in `v0.300.0..main` — **94.20%** — were captured CI transcripts and lockfiles that this repository *requires* each iteration to commit. Committing the evidence the process demands was itself scored as a regression, so the ratchet could only ever tighten past reach. Artifacts (`*.log`, `*.jsonl`, `*.diff`, `*.patch`, `*.stderr`, `*.stdout`) and lockfiles are now excluded symmetrically from both sides of the ratio, under a new `METRIC_VERSION = 2` so the change starts a fresh comparison epoch instead of silently invalidating recorded history.\n- *Enforcement sat where nobody could act on it.* It ran in `record_release`, on `main`, after every contributing commit was already immutable — and it blocked the only mechanism that advances the range. Release-time is now `RatchetPolicy::Report`; enforcement moved to a differential (branch-vs-base) pull-request gate, where the commits can still be amended.\n\nVerified: the exact command that failed in CI now exits 0 — `4.36% (1608/36896 changed lines; 10/119 commits)`.\n\n**2. macOS desktop codesigning.** Not a packaging defect. Run 29752745259 is a `workflow_run` *healing* build of tag `v0.300.0`, which predates the four packaging fixes already merged; the fixed code is proven green in PR run 29746221627 (`considered=1479 skipped=751`). It needs a newer tag, which fix 1 unblocks. Rather than leave this to be re-diagnosed every time, `scripts/desktop-release-resolve.sh` now emits a `::notice` when it is packaging a tag that is not the tip of the default branch.\n\n## Checks that never gated\n\n| What | Why it was green |\n| --- | --- |\n| `cargo clippy` | Ran without `-D warnings`. Every lint in `[lints.clippy]` is `warn`, so the job printed findings and exited 0. The tree is clean today, so adding it promotes existing behaviour rather than introducing failures. |\n| `scripts/check-file-size.rs` | Matched directory exclusions as **substrings**, so `.git` hid all of `.github/workflows/**`. Exclusions are now matched per path component; workflows are measured (warn 1500, fail 2000), while third-party workflows quoted under `docs/case-studies/**` stay exempt — reformatting them would destroy the thing they document. |\n| workflow definitions and shell scripts | Nothing validated either. A mistyped `needs.<job>` reference fails **open**: the guarded step silently skips under a green check. `actionlint` 1.7.7 (shellcheck-embedded) and `shellcheck --severity=warning` now run in `lint`. |\n| desktop library tests | `node --test $(ls … \\| grep -v …)` fell back to Node's own discovery whenever the glob stopped matching — a green step running none of the intended tests. The list is built explicitly and an empty list is a hard error. |\n\n## Checks that gated the wrong thing\n\n- `auto-release`/`manual-release` needed only `[lint, test, build]`. A red **`Secrets Scan`** or either E2E suite on `main` still published the crate, the Docker image and the GitHub Release. (`docker-build` is deliberately *not* a gate: it publishes independently and its failure must not block the crate.)\n- The desktop `finalize` job ran under `always()`, so a **cancelled** run could `gh release upload --clobber` a partial `SHA256SUMS.txt` over a complete one. Now `!cancelled()`.\n- Desktop packaging and VS Code jobs validated a stale merge preview; they now simulate the fresh merge, as `lint`/`test` already did.\n- `secretlint` floated to whatever `latest` was at run time. Pinned.\n- `$BASE_REF` was unquoted in `scripts/simulate-fresh-merge.sh` — a word-split there takes the *skip* branch and reports success without merging.\n- `upload-pages-artifact` dropped dotfiles (`include-hidden-files: true`); the screenshot upload was `if: always()` with the default `if-no-files-found: warn`, i.e. green on an empty artifact.\n\nOne finding was deliberately **not** \"fixed\": `scripts/install.sh` is the only script without `pipefail`, because it is the only one running under `#!/usr/bin/env sh`. `pipefail` is not POSIX and aborts dash — the `/bin/sh` a `curl … | sh` install lands in. The rationale is now recorded in the file header so it is not \"fixed\" later.\n\n## Upstream reports\n\nThree defects reproduce in the templates themselves; all were re-verified against upstream `main` via the API before reporting.\n\n- link-foundation/js-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template#111 — **filed in this iteration**: unquoted `$BASE_REF` in `simulate-fresh-merge.sh`, with a reproduction, a workaround and a suggested fix. (The rust template's copy quotes correctly; python does not ship the script.)\n- link-foundation/python-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template#33 and #35 — already open upstream, so recorded rather than re-filed.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `cargo test --test unit` → **1953 passed, 0 failed** (6 new regression tests)\n- `actionlint -shellcheck` over every workflow → exit 0 (type-checking confirmed live by injecting a bogus `needs.secrets-scanX` into a copy and observing the error)\n- `shellcheck --severity=warning` over the 17 shipped scripts → exit 0\n- `cargo fmt --all -- --check`, `cargo clippy --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings`, `rust-script scripts/check-file-size.rs` → exit 0\n\nNew tests: `releases_do_not_publish_past_a_failing_secrets_scan_or_e2e_suite`, `lint_job_gates_on_workflow_shell_and_clippy_findings`, `check_directory_measures_github_workflows`, `check_directory_does_not_measure_quoted_case_study_workflows`, plus the ratchet exclusion and differential-gate tests.\n\n## Debug output (default off, per the issue)\n\n- `FORMAL_AI_MACOS_SIGN_DEBUG=1` — per-path `[adhoc-sign-mac]` sign decisions; its *absence* from the failing log is what proved the healing-build diagnosis.\n- `experiments/self_hosting_ratchet_replay/replay.py` — per-commit attribution, reason and subject for any range, without `rust-script`.\n\n## Second pass: two defects found by running the fixed pipeline\n\nRun [29767811026](https://github.com/link-assistant/formal-ai/actions/runs/29767811026) was the first full run of the repaired pipeline, and it found two more — one introduced by this PR's own first commit.\n\n**`Test (ubuntu-latest)` reported failure with 1953 tests passed and 0 failed.** The suite finished at 18:43:50; `timeout-minutes: 15` killed the job 1.1 seconds later, during teardown. Run 29749095334 on `main` had already done exactly this and was never diagnosed, because GitHub renders a timeout as `cancelled` — indistinguishable from an infrastructure blip. The budget was not padding: checkout plus the two data checks cost ~3m15s before a test runs, the suite takes ~11m, and eight individual agentic/driver tests each exceed 60 s. Recent `main` durations show creep rather than a one-off — 11m02s, 12m27s, 11m49s, 12m57s, **15m16s (cancelled)**, 14m13s. The budget now matches measured cost, and the step emits a `::warning` past 70% of it so the margin cannot be eaten again in silence. Making those eight tests cheaper is separate work with its own regression risk; what is fixed here is the false report.\n\n**The release gates I added would have accepted a timed-out job.** They read `needs.<job>.result != 'failure'`, chosen so a legitimately *skipped* job would not block a release — but the finding above is the proof that a timeout resolves to `'cancelled'`, which is also `!= 'failure'`. Both gates now enumerate the two acceptable results, and the regression test asserts the positive form *and* that the weaker spelling is absent.\n\nThat run also independently confirmed the rest: `Self-Hosting Evidence Check` green **on the real PR**, every new lint step green, and [Desktop Release 29767811373](https://github.com/link-assistant/formal-ai/actions/runs/29767811373) green across all six platform legs including both macOS architectures — the confirmation that current desktop code signs and packages fine, and that run 29752745259 failed only because it was a healing build of the older `v0.300.0` tag.",
